post_install() {
  systemctl --global enable hearthdeck.target hearthdeck-kiosk.service
  if ! grep -qxF 'Include = /etc/pacman.d/hearthdeck.conf' /etc/pacman.conf; then
    cat >>/etc/pacman.conf <<'EOF'

# BEGIN HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY
Include = /etc/pacman.d/hearthdeck.conf
# END HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY
EOF
  fi

  cat <<'EOF'
Hearthdeck installs systemd user units for its API daemon, host bridge, and
COSMIC Kiosk launcher.

The local-service target starts for users at their next login. The Kiosk client
starts only through the Hearthdeck Kiosk COSMIC session. The Hearthdeck launcher
also starts local services for the current user. To start them immediately
without launching Hearthdeck, run as that user:

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user start hearthdeck.target

The launcher pairs with the loopback daemon automatically. Optional LAN/TLS
configuration belongs in ~/.config/hearthdeck/daemon.env; see
/usr/share/doc/hearthdeck/daemon.env.example.

Future Hearthdeck package updates arrive through `pacman -Syu`. The GitHub
Pages repository is unsigned and should not be enabled on systems that require
signed packages.

If Hearthdeck was previously installed from a source checkout, move its copied
unit files out of ~/.config/systemd/user/ first. Those files override the
package units in /usr/lib/systemd/user. Preserve customizations as drop-ins.
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

pre_remove() {
  sed -i '/^# BEGIN HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY$/,/^# END HEARTHDECK PACMAN REPOSITORY$/d' /etc/pacman.conf
}

post_remove() {
  systemctl --global disable hearthdeck.target hearthdeck-kiosk.service

  cat <<'EOF'
If this was the final Hearthdeck package removal, disable its user services:
  systemctl --user disable --now hearthdeck.target hearthdeck-bridge.socket \
    hearthdeck-kiosk.service
EOF
}
